# Expectation record for 01_integer_conversions/007_enum_value_arithmetic.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 007_enum_value_arithmetic
area               = 01_integer_conversions
description        = Enumeration constants in arithmetic and enumerated-type underlying behaviour, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every enumerator is small and every arithmetic result is far inside the range of int, so no
signed type overflows: the largest magnitude occurring anywhere in the program, printed or
intermediate, is 300. No shift operator appears at all, so no shift count can be out of
range.
Both array subscripts are known enumerators inside the bounds of a three element array, and
the two forms differ deliberately: the folded subscript is the enumeration constant SEQ_C,
which is 2, while the runtime subscript is read from a volatile object holding SEQ_B, which
is 1. Neither can reach index 3, so no out-of-bounds access occurs and AddressSanitizer
stays clean.
The switch in classify covers all three enumerators of enum Color and is still followed by a
return statement, so no path falls off the end of a non-void function whatever value reaches
it.
Several calls read more than one volatile object in a single argument list, and one of them
-- the run_switch line -- does so through three function calls. That is deliberate and it is
not undefined. Every access is a read, every read is of a different object, and no object is
modified anywhere, so no side effect is unsequenced relative to another side effect on the
same object or to a value computation using that object, which is the condition C11 6.5p2
actually states; and the three function calls are indeterminately sequenced with respect to
one another rather than unsequenced (C11 6.5.2.2p10). Because the accesses touch distinct
objects and none of them writes, the printed values are identical whichever order an
implementation chooses, so this program does not depend on unspecified evaluation order
either -- a separate obligation from undefined-behaviour freedom, and one a differential
oracle needs just as much.
Otherwise: no aliasing violation, no uninitialized read -- every volatile object and the
table are initialized in their own declarations -- and no dependence on padding bytes or on
the addresses of unrelated objects.
Every volatile enumerated value that reaches printf does so through an explicit int cast --
or, on the run_switch line, through classify's prototyped enum Color parameter and its int
return -- and the size relation is printed through a cast as well, so the type of every
printed value is fixed at the call site; the enumeration constants already have type int and
need no cast. The complete default warning gate passes on this program with no diagnostic and
no deviation, measured with gcc 13.4.0, which is why this record declares no ub_audit_flags
at all.
The three _Generic controlling expressions are not evaluated (C11 6.5.1.1p3), so they report
types without performing a conversion or a volatile access at run time.
Every arithmetic property is computed twice, once from enumeration constants the folder can
evaluate at compile time and once from volatile-qualified storage that forces a run-time
read, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the one the backend computes. The
size relation is the single property with no runtime twin, because sizeof is evaluated at
compile time by definition.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Two implementation-defined properties are navigated by authoring technique rather than
tested naively, and neither is a reason to restrict the target list or to switch an oracle
off: all four targets stay fully compared under all three oracles.
First, the enumerated type's compatible type. C11 6.7.2.2p4 leaves the type compatible with
an enumerated type implementation-defined, so listing both enum Color and int among the
associations of one _Generic is a constraint violation wherever those two turn out to be
compatible -- gcc 13.4.0 rejects that form outright with "_Generic specifies two compatible
types", measured on this program's own enumeration and its volatile enum Color object. The
portable form used here therefore lists only the enumerated type itself plus default. The
two probes that do report int are portable for reasons that do not depend on the compatible
type at all: an identifier declared as an enumeration constant has type int by C11 6.4.4.3p2,
which is what makes the RED probe report int, and int can represent every value of enum
Color, so the integer promotions give a + 0 the type int by C11 6.3.1.1p2 whatever the
compatible type happens to be.
Second, the enumerated type's width, which follows from that same implementation-defined
choice. It is never printed as a number. It is asserted only as the relation
sizeof(enum Color) == sizeof(int), printed as a 0/1 comparison, and that relation was
measured to hold on all four supported targets, both sizes being 4, which is what makes the
line comparable across backends. Printing the width itself would risk reporting a purely
implementation-defined difference as a finding against a backend that did nothing wrong.
The negative enumerator BLUE = -3 removes a further class of variation by forcing the
compatible type to be signed, and that was verified rather than assumed: (enum Color)-1 < 0
was measured true on all four targets, while the same test applied to a control enumeration
whose enumerators are all non-negative was measured false. The sign of the compatible type
therefore follows the enumerator values, as the standard requires, and does not vary by
target here.
No plain char object is declared and no char value is ever read or printed, because plain
char signedness was measured signed on x86-64 and i686 but unsigned on AArch64 and
RISC-V 64. The type char appears only as the pointed-to type of printf's format parameter
and of the string literals the three type probes return, where its signedness cannot matter
because no element of either is ever read as a value by this program. long and unsigned long
are never used at all, because their width was measured as 4 bytes on i686 and 8 bytes on the
other three targets. No pointer value is printed: the probes return string literals, which %s
dereferences rather than printing as addresses.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fold_values=1 5 -3
fold_seq=0 1 2
fold_gap=10 11 20 21
fold_difference=4
fold_product=-6
fold_total=3
fold_index=33
fold_relation=1
fold_size_relation=1
run_values=1 5 -3
run_seq=1
run_gap=11 21
run_difference=4
run_product=-6
run_total=3
run_index=22
run_relation=1
run_switch=100 200 300
enumerator_type=int
enum_object_type=enum Color
enum_sum_type=int
END
